Rutgers band director Tim Smith told NJ Advance Media it is something of an unofficial tradition for Big Ten marching bands to play the visiting school's fight song.
"It is not something we started," Smith said. "We picked this up when we visited other schools in the Big Ten. We travel with a small group of kids to several (road) games every year and it happened at Wisconsin, where members of their band came over to our fan section and played our fight song. It happened at Ohio State, Indiana."
Rutgers, which has become a too-easy punchline for national media because of the events of recent years, was criticized on Twitter and on TheSpun.com, which wrote "apparently, it's gotten so bad, the team's own band decided to stir things up a bit."
According to some Tweets and eyewitness accounts, some remaining Rutgers fans started yelling obscenities at the home band for its act of courtesy to Michigan fans, who drank in the celebratory moment.
"It is a tradition of the bands to play the visitor's fight song for every visiting team during our pregame show," Smith said, "and then in the third quarter we send a small group out into the stands to play our fight song up close for our fans.
"Then when we happen to go by the visitor's section, we will also play a short version of their fight song as a matter of welcoming them and showing some hospitality to those folks when they come to our stadium."
After Rutgers marching band played Fight Song for Michigan fans, their own fans screamed obscenities at them #MICHvsRUTG #classy #goblue -- sharontbaum (@sharontbaum) October 9, 2016
This is the final football season for Smith, who is retiring after 21 years at Rutgers, including 18 as leader of the 225-person band ensemble.
"The fact of the matter is those kids in our band and myself and my staff live and die with team on the sidelines," Smith said. "We felt terrible about it, but the two things are completely separate. Us being gracious hosts for the visiting fans and our team not having a great night are mutually exclusive."
