It's difficult not to tackle a two-headed monster high
Published: May 8 2012 - 3:00AM
IN TRYING, honourably, to deal with one problem, the AFL inadvertently has created two more, each in its own way acute. Anxious to present the game as hard rather than brutal, the AFL all but banned even the most incidental contact to the head, Andrew Demetriou declaring it ''sacrosanct''. The judiciary was uncompromising in its reinforcement.
At first, players were hesitant, even confused. But professional sportspeople are nothing if not ready to exploit opportunity. This season, two trends have emerged. One is for footballers to play for head high contact, by wriggling earthward when tackled.
Some players are naturally good at it. Geelong captain Joel Selwood is one and is unapologetic about it. Some clubs are coaching it. West Coast is one; statistics show it graphically. North Melbourne's Brad Scott and Hawthorn's Alastair Clarkson went as far as they dared in post-match reflections without antagonising West Coast or incurring a fine.
It is not the only reason that the Eagles are unbeaten, but it is going to make them that much harder to beat.
Scott said countering the simulators is about technique. So do others coaches. Essentially, that means going in lower than your opponent. That conjures up images of AFL as non-stop limbo. In any case, it can only work to a certain extent. Too low and the issue becomes tripping.
The other development is for some players to hurtle into packs, headlong and recklessly, certain of violent contact, but also certain of a free kick. There were many instances on the weekend. The players believe they are protected by the laws. In the moment, they are. But they are not protected against crippling injury, as Matthew Lloyd warned yesterday.
Nor will they be retrospectively protected if in 10 years time they are discovered to have a degenerative brain injury.
