Holder also dances with BRB (he played a brilliantly bored chap in Ashton's Façade). For Quatrain, he picked a Piazzolla score, arranged by Leonid Desyatnikov and excitingly played live, and responds to its bite and languor. Adam Wiltshire's nest of steel trapeziums throws shadows against the wall, and four shirtless men drive the piece – the women embellish or curl up against them. Couples wind through ominous strings, especially in a solemn, faltering duet for Céline Gittens and Yasuo Atsuji. They bring a grown-up sense of melancholy, their haughty lines etching the air.
While Holder makes his cast look good, Whitley makes his look busy. Kin. never stops. A nameless crisis takes hold: the score keeps running, the dancers can't settle. To a feverish, driving string quartet by US composer Phil Kline, the ceaseless movement for dancers in inky costumes feels almost neurotic. A tremulous Jenna Roberts opens the piece squiggling in the half-light, reaching, collapsing. She wraps long arms around herself, trails them through space, but can't put her anxious body on hold – Joseph Caley has to plant himself in her path, and their duet only ends when they fall to the floor, exhausted.{g}
