NEWARK -- Forget the bubble. Forget sweating out Selection Sunday. Seton Hall can book its trip to the NCAA Tournament for the first time since 2006, and after dominating No. 5 Xavier at the Prudential Center on Sunday afternoon, the Pirates look capable of doing some damage when they get there.
They opened up a 9-0 lead on the Musketeers, who brought Final Four hopes and a 25-3 record to New Jersey, and never looked back in a 90-81 victory. Even in a game when star guard Isaiah Whitehead struggled early, the Pirates were the better team in nearly every facet.
Forward Desi Rodriguez had a double-double with a career-high 27 points on 9-of-16 shooting and 12 rebounds. Whitehead bounced back with 22 points, including 15 in the second half, as the sold-out home crowd gave the players a standing ovation after their final home game.
The victory improves Seton Hall to 21-7 and 11-5 in the Big East with two road games remaining at Butler and DePaul, before the conference tournament. The Pirates are playing for a better seed now, because the victory over Xavier is the "signature win" that the Pirates' tournament resume has lacked.
How Derrick Gordon can be a trailblazer again
THREE OBSERVATIONS
1. Rodriguez was a monster. His tomahawk dunk early in the second half nearly blew the roof off the Prudential Center, but one highlight isn't enough to sum up his brilliant game. He was dominant up front during his 36 minutes, and did so against arguably the toughest and deepest frontcourt in the Big East. Well done.
2. Rebounding with the best. Xavier came into the game as the Big East's best rebounding team and 13th nationally in rebounding margin, topping opponents by an average of 8.2 boards a game. The Pirates not only held their own on the glass, but held a 28-18 advantage in the first half (it was 44-39 at the end). That was an impressive accomplishment, and maybe the biggest reason this team could build such a big early lead.
3. This is not a one-man team. The Musketeers made it clear from the beginning that it wasn't going to let Isaiah Whitehead, who torched Providence three nights earlier on this same court for 25 points, beat them. He struggled against a steady diet of double teams. Still, the Pirates dominated, and that has to be a good sign for this team come March. (Oh, and Whitehead had two huge 3-pointers in the second half and still finished with 22 points anyway.)
TURNING POINT
Really, with the Pirates up 9-0 in a matter of minutes, it's hard to pinpoint just one. But after Rodriguez wowed the crowd with his tomahawk, he found teammate Angel Delgado with a sweet pass on a fast break to give Seton Hall a 54-36 lead. Xavier trimmed that advantage a few times, but it never felt like the Pirates were going to let them back into the game.
Whitehead can make the Pirates an NCAA force
SENIOR MOMENT
