In his lab at the University of Texas at Dallas, Baughman is putting a new spin on yarn: He can create flexible yarns with a variety of functions by spraying carbon threads with nanoparticles and then twisting the threads together. A yarn filled with a lithium dust, for example, can generate an electrical current. Pair it with a graphite-filled yarn for an anode, and you could create a knittable, knotable, high-performance battery.
