Tips from friends and family will always be more compelling than some computer intoning 'we have recommendations for you'
Tanned and rested after a week's holiday in Cuba, my colleague cornered me by my desk. Bracing myself for tales of the post-Castro regime and drunken antics with Canadian backpackers, I asked him how it went. He looked back at me wide-eyed. "I loved those books you recommended," he said. "The Raw Shark Texts was just amazing."
Over the next 10 minutes I learnt nothing of Cuba, but plenty about his theories on Jim Dodge's Stone Junction. Since returning, he'd already ordered all of Dodge's books and was badgering me for more recommendations. I was flattered and slightly jealous that Dodge's incandescent novel was still burning bright in his mind.
Recommending books to friends and family can be a most gratifying process. Not only are they grateful that you've taken the trouble to think what they might like, but also you get to look differently at the books you've enjoyed. Things are altered when refracted through their eyes, like when you introduce a new partner to your hometown for the first time. For a good friend's birthday, for example, I toyed with the idea of Donald Ray Pollock's tales of deadbeats and deviants, Knockemstiff. To me it's an arresting and consistently stunning first book, but "visiting" it with my friend in tow made it seem a little too keen to shock. The Ministry of Special Cases by Nathan Englander, however, was a snug fit.
It's crucial to remember that their enjoyment is key - it's not a time to show off how literary, hip or intelligent you are. No one will thank you for getting all Alain de Botton when they're saddled with three esoteric and impossibly difficult books when all they wanted was something to absorb them as they fried on the beach. Push them towards the best of whatever genre they like, but don't ever recommend something that you think is "good" for them.
After many years of trying to "improve" my mother's taste in novels, I gave up and started recommending books that I thought she might actually like. After some research - including 50 pages of Barbara Taylor Bradford's sanity-defying, eye-bleedingly written, astonishingly awful The Ravenscar Dynasty - I recently bought her Just a Family Affair by Veronica Henry, a witty and jaunty bonkbuster. She passed it from friend to friend, with glowing reviews, making it probably the hottest book amongst her circle since Shirley Conran's Savages.
As she excitedly told me about how much she loved Henry's book, I remembered the brief and gratifying conversations I had as a bookseller with customers who'd enjoyed one of my recommendations. While their thanks didn't quite make up for being constantly skint, it did at least remind me that in a very tiny way these tips could change people's lives.
And now, years later and without a commercial imperative to do so, I still continue to enthuse about new books and old discoveries, still finish a book and immediately wonder who I could pass it on to. In spite of Amazon's complex algorithms and Shelfari, personal recommendations are still the best way to encounter new novels and writers. So while I can whole-heartedly recommend{r} a{g} book{r} to{r} friends{g} and{r} family{r},{g} I{r}'m{r} not{g} sure{g} I{r} can{r} recommend{g} it{g} to{g} anyone{r} else{r}.{r}
